# Reproducing TCKDB dataset release ${tag}

This directory is a `tckdb.deposit.v1` deposit. `MANIFEST.json` lists every
file here with its SHA-256; nothing in this protocol needs anything that is
not listed there or in the pinned source checkout.

Bound source:

| what | value |
| --- | --- |
| git commit | `${git_commit}` |
| git tag | `${git_tag}` |
| `tckdb-backend` | `${backend_version}` |
| `tckdb-schemas` | `${schemas_package_version}` |
| Alembic head | `${alembic_head}` |

Definitions. A **release** is the frozen, citable set of selected values and
their candidates (`release/`). The **archive** (`${archive_member}`) is the
lossless database snapshot that carries the evidence behind the release. A
**generator** is a script that reads the restored database and emits one
manuscript number; `expected_outputs/` holds what each generator produced on
the instance the release was cut from.

## 0. Check the deposit's own integrity (no database, no checkout needed)

Run from inside the deposit directory. `sha256sum -c` expects two spaces
between digest and path; the one-liner prints exactly that.

```bash
python3 -c 'import json; print("\n".join(m["sha256"] + "  " + m["path"] for m in json.load(open("MANIFEST.json"))["members"]))' | sha256sum -c -
```

Every member must print `OK`. With the checkout available,
`build_publication_deposit.py checksums <deposit>` prints the same list.

## 1. Check out the pinned commit

```bash
git clone https://github.com/TCKDB/TCKDB.git tckdb && cd tckdb
git checkout ${git_commit}
git describe --tags --exact-match HEAD   # prints ${git_tag}
```

Create the environment from the pinned `source/environment.yml` (or the
`source/Dockerfile`), then install the two packages so their versions are
known:

```bash
conda env create -f <deposit>/source/environment.yml -n tckdb_env
conda run -n tckdb_env python -m pip install -e schemas/python/tckdb-schemas
conda run -n tckdb_env python -m pip install -e "backend[dev]"
```

## 2. Start an empty database and object store, migrate to head

```bash
docker compose up -d db seaweedfs
```

The backend reads its database from these environment variables
(`Settings.database_url`, `backend/app/api/config.py`): `DB_USER`,
`DB_PASSWORD`, `DB_HOST`, `DB_PORT`, `DB_NAME`. The object store that holds
restored calculation artifacts is read by `backend/app/services/artifact_storage.py`
from `S3_ENDPOINT_URL`, `S3_ACCESS_KEY`, `S3_SECRET_KEY`, `S3_BUCKET`,
`S3_REGION`. The `docker compose` defaults match the values below.

```bash
export DB_USER=tckdb DB_PASSWORD=tckdb DB_HOST=127.0.0.1 DB_PORT=5432 DB_NAME=tckdb_repro
export S3_ENDPOINT_URL=http://localhost:9000 S3_ACCESS_KEY=tckdb S3_SECRET_KEY=tckdb_secret S3_BUCKET=tckdb-artifacts S3_REGION=us-east-1
PGPASSWORD=$$DB_PASSWORD createdb -h $$DB_HOST -U $$DB_USER $$DB_NAME
cd backend && conda run -n tckdb_env alembic upgrade head && cd ..
```

`alembic current` must print `${alembic_head}`. The restore refuses any
other revision.

## 3. Verify and restore the evidence archive

```bash
conda run -n tckdb_env python backend/scripts/tckdb_archive.py verify <deposit>/${archive_member}
conda run -n tckdb_env python backend/scripts/tckdb_archive.py restore <deposit>/${archive_member}
```

The restore requires an otherwise empty database at exactly the revision
above. It writes every calculation artifact into the object store and every
row into the database, byte-exact.

## 4. Regenerate the manuscript numbers and byte-diff them

```bash
conda run -n tckdb_env python backend/scripts/paper/generate_expected_outputs.py --output-dir /tmp/regenerated
diff -r <deposit>/expected_outputs /tmp/regenerated && echo "expected outputs reproduced byte for byte"
```

Every `[DATA]` number in the manuscript is read from one of these files.

## 5. Verify the deposit against the restored database

```bash
conda run -n tckdb_env python backend/scripts/ops/build_publication_deposit.py verify <deposit> --db
conda run -n tckdb_env python backend/scripts/ops/verify_artifact_integrity.py --release ${tag}
```

The first re-hashes every member, checks the archive against its own
manifest, and confirms the restored database holds the same frozen release
bytes, the same Alembic revision and no account outside `ACCOUNTS.md`. The
second re-reads every artifact the release cites from the object store and
checks each against its recorded digest.

## What a clean run proves, and what it does not

A clean run proves that the deposited bytes are internally consistent, that
the database they describe can be rebuilt from them, and that the numbers
the manuscript quotes are what the deposited scripts compute from that
database. It does not prove that any value is chemically accurate: the
release's curation policy, review history and candidate set are in
`release/` precisely so that a reader can disagree with a selection.
